// Firestore security rules.
//
// ─── APPEND-ONLY COLLECTIONS ────────────────────────────────────────────────
//   users/{uid}/lineageEvents/{eventId}
//   users/{uid}/icpProfiles/{icpId}/criteriaVersions/{versionId}
//
// These are Sprint 1A's history records. Create is allowed; update and delete
// are not, so "append-only" is enforced by the database rather than by every
// call site remembering. icpRelationshipService also checks existence inside
// its transaction, but that is defence in depth, not the guarantee.
//
// ─── TWO THINGS THAT LOOK CORRECT AND ARE NOT ───────────────────────────────
// Both were caught by running the suite in scripts/rules-check against a real
// emulator. Neither would have failed at deploy time; the first would have
// denied writes for every user.
//
//   1. `document.size()` DOES NOT EXIST for a recursive-wildcard binding.
//      The emulator reports "Function not found error: Name: [size]", and a
//      rule whose expression errors evaluates to DENY — so a size()-based guard
//      does not merely fail to protect, it denies ordinary writes. That is why
//      there is no path introspection anywhere below: `coll` and `sub` are
//      SINGLE-segment wildcards, which bind plain strings and compare directly.
//
//   2. A RECURSIVE WILDCARD MATCHES ZERO OR MORE SEGMENTS, so the deep
//      `/{coll}/{docId}/{rest=**}` block also matches 2-deep documents. Firestore
//      grants access if ANY matching rule allows it, so every guard above has to
//      be repeated there. Omitting one silently re-grants what was withheld.
//
// ─── SERVER-CONTROLLED FIELDS ON users/{uid} ────────────────────────────────
// The owner may write their own user document, but not the fields the server
// trusts for authorization or that record billing. Each is written only by the
// Admin SDK, which bypasses these rules:
//   role                       admin / super_admin access (utils/adminAuth.js,
//                              utils/superAdminAuth.js, verifyAuthToken.js)
//   permissions, permissionsOverride, permissions_reset_at
//                              super-admin permission set (superAdminAuth.js,
//                              superAdminRepairTools.js)
//   accountStatus, suspended*, reactivated*
//                              suspension state (adminSuspendAccount.js,
//                              adminReactivateAccount.js)
//   hasCompletedPayment, paymentCompletedAt, subscriptionTier,
//   subscriptionStatus, selectedTier, status, credits, monthlyCredits,
//   tierLimits, features, billingCycleDate, stripeCustomerId,
//   stripeSubscriptionId       billing (stripe-webhook.js; Signup.jsx at create)
//   currentPeriodStart, currentPeriodEnd, cancelAtPeriodEnd, pauseCollection,
//   billingDate, nextBillingDate
//                              billing period/cancellation state (billing
//                              hardening PR 1 — stripe-webhook.js). billingDate/
//                              nextBillingDate are legacy field names nothing
//                              writes anymore; protected anyway, closing a gap
//                              where they were the two billing fields NOT on
//                              this list even though the UI reads them.
//   pendingCheckout            checkout claim/recovery state (billing hardening
//                              PR 1 — create-checkout-session.js). One field
//                              holding a nested object; protecting the field
//                              protects everything nested in it (operationId,
//                              status, claimedAt — a client that could write
//                              this could forge a session URL or clear another
//                              request's in-flight claim).
// One client write is allowed: Signup.jsx creates the document with six of the
// billing fields, only on a supported tier and only with the values it writes
// (see signupTiers and signupBilling) — either the legacy numeric `credits`
// shape or the new object shape, both strictly validated and BOTH still
// accepted (not just during a transition window: there is no reliable signal
// from here that every old frontend bundle is gone, so the legacy branch stays
// until there's actual evidence it's safe to remove).
// Deleting the user document is not allowed from a client.
//
// ─── STICKERS (docs/adr/ADR-007-native-stickers.md) ─────────────────────────
//   users/{uid}/stickers/{stickerId}    the user's sticker definitions
//   users/{uid}/contacts/{id}           sticker_ids, sticker_assignments and
//                                       sticker_change: a contact's membership
//   feature_flags/stickers              the rollout mode (server-only)
//   sticker_enrollment/{uid}            the beta list (server-only)
// Every sticker write needs the rollout mode to allow this user, read here
// from feature_flags/stickers so these rules and the stickersConfig function
// give the same answer (src/constants/stickersAccess.js holds the table both
// suites test). Stickers are never deleted. A membership write changes
// exactly one sticker, names it in sticker_change, and may only add a sticker
// that exists, belongs to the same user and is active.
//
// ROLLBACK NEVER BROADENS. Do not revert this section: the generic blocks
// below would then let the owner write stickers and membership without any
// of these checks. To turn every sticker write off, set
// feature_flags/stickers.mode to "off" (no deploy). If the rules themselves
// are at fault, make stickerRulesEnabled() return false and deploy: that
// denies every sticker write and changes nothing else.
// scripts/rules-check/stickers.mjs tests both.

// ─── BEFORE CHANGING THIS FILE ──────────────────────────────────────────────
// CI runs the suite in scripts/rules-check on every PR. Run it locally
// too — it needs a JDK 21+ and takes under a minute. Case 09 is the one that
// matters most: `update users/U/companies/c1 → ALLOW`. The likely failure mode
// of a bad rule is that EVERY product write denies, not that the protected
// collections stay mutable.

rules_version = '2';
service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {

    function owns(userId) {
      return request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == userId;
    }

    // Fields on users/{uid} that only the server may set (header above).
    function serverControlledUserFields() {
      return ['role', 'permissions', 'permissionsOverride', 'permissions_reset_at',
              'accountStatus', 'suspendedAt', 'suspendedBy', 'suspensionReason',
              'reactivatedAt', 'reactivatedBy', 'reactivationReason',
              'hasCompletedPayment', 'paymentCompletedAt', 'subscriptionTier',
              'subscriptionStatus', 'selectedTier', 'status', 'credits',
              'monthlyCredits', 'tierLimits', 'features', 'billingCycleDate',
              'stripeCustomerId', 'stripeSubscriptionId',
              'currentPeriodStart', 'currentPeriodEnd', 'cancelAtPeriodEnd',
              'pauseCollection', 'billingDate', 'nextBillingDate', 'pendingCheckout', 'billingLock',
              'myCardPublishBlocked'];
    }

    // The billing fields Signup.jsx writes when it creates the document.
    function signupBillingFields() {
      return ['selectedTier', 'subscriptionTier', 'status',
              'hasCompletedPayment', 'credits', 'monthlyCredits'];
    }

    // The tiers a user can sign up for: the two the homepage links to
    // (Homepage.jsx), the two CheckoutPage.jsx has a price and a payment link
    // for, and the two stripe-webhook.js maps a payment to.
    function signupTiers() {
      return ['starter', 'pro'];
    }

    // The two credit allocations a signup is allowed to start with.
    function signupCreditAmount(tier) {
      return tier == 'pro' ? 1250 : 400;
    }

    // Legacy shape: `credits` as a bare number. Unchanged from the original rule.
    function signupBillingLegacyNumber(data) {
      return data.credits == signupCreditAmount(data.selectedTier);
    }

    // New shape: `credits` as an object with known history. Only these four keys,
    // used must be exactly 0 (a fresh signup has used nothing), remaining must equal
    // total, total must be the tier's exact allocation, and resetDate must be null
    // (a pending_payment signup has no billing cycle yet — a client should not be
    // able to set anything else here).
    function signupBillingObjectShape(data) {
      return data.credits.keys().hasOnly(['total', 'used', 'remaining', 'resetDate'])
        && data.credits.used == 0
        && data.credits.total == signupCreditAmount(data.selectedTier)
        && data.credits.remaining == data.credits.total
        && data.credits.resetDate == null;
    }

    // Exactly what Signup.jsx writes: an unpaid account on a supported tier, with the
    // credits that tier starts with — either shape, both strictly validated. Kept
    // dual-accept indefinitely (not for a fixed window): an already-open browser tab
    // running the old bundle can still submit the legacy shape after a deploy, and
    // there's no reliable signal from here that every old tab is gone.
    function signupBilling(data) {
      return data.keys().hasAll(signupBillingFields())
        && data.status == 'pending_payment'
        && data.hasCompletedPayment == false
        && data.selectedTier in signupTiers()
        && data.subscriptionTier == data.selectedTier
        && data.monthlyCredits == signupCreditAmount(data.selectedTier)
        && (signupBillingLegacyNumber(data) || signupBillingObjectShape(data));
    }

    // Integration documents only the server may write (see the users block).
    function serverOwnedIntegration(coll, docId) {
      return coll == 'integrations' && docId == 'linkedin';
    }

    // Billing W4: the credit log, per-request charge records and daily search
    // counts are written only by the server's credit metering (Admin SDK).
    // The owner may read them; no client may create, change or delete them.
    function serverOwnedCredits(coll) {
      return coll == 'creditTransactions' || coll == 'creditCharges' || coll == 'creditUsage';
    }

    // ── My Card (users/{uid}/card/main) ─────────────────────────────────────
    //
    // The owner's own card, written only by src/services/myCard/myCardCore.js.
    // These rules are the server-side half of what that service does: the card
    // has exactly its known fields, each within its limit; sharing stays off;
    // and a write lands only as the NEXT revision of the card it replaces, so
    // a stale tab, an old build or a direct write cannot roll a newer card
    // back. Limits mirror CARD_LIMITS in src/utils/myCardRules.js: change one,
    // change the other (scripts/rules-check/my-card.mjs checks the limits).
    //
    // What they cannot do: updatedAt is the client's clock, so only its shape
    // is checked; and an owner can still edit their OWN card, which is the
    // point. The card is private: nothing here makes it readable by anyone else.
    //
    // photoUrl is one of three things: nothing, an https link, or a small JPEG
    // kept in the card as a data URL (made in the browser by utils/photoPrep.js).
    // The picture is bounded tightly: exactly the JPEG data-URL prefix, then
    // "/9j/" (every JPEG begins FF D8 FF, which is that in base64), then base64
    // only, whose length is a multiple of 4, in 200 to 80,000 characters in all.
    // The client cap is 65,536 (PHOTO.MAX_DATA_URL_CHARS); this is the margin
    // above it. Both are PROVISIONAL. Rules cannot walk a JPEG, so that the bytes
    // are a real, square, metadata-free picture is the client's check
    // (inspectPhotoDataUrl), not this rule's.

    function cardKeys() {
      return ['firstName', 'lastName', 'photoUrl', 'title', 'organization', 'email', 'phone',
              'linkedinUrl', 'instagram', 'website', 'share', 'sharingEnabled',
              'schemaVersion', 'revision', 'createdAt', 'updatedAt'];
    }

    function cardShareKeys() {
      return ['photoUrl', 'title', 'organization', 'email', 'phone', 'linkedinUrl', 'instagram', 'website'];
    }

    // ── My Card sharing (schema version 2; MY-CARD-SHARE-001) ───────────────
    //
    // A version 2 card may also hold a publicId and the time it was first published, and up to
    // five additional links (link1..link5, each with its own Shared/Private switch in `share`).
    // Fixed slots, not a list, because rules cannot loop: each slot gets the same field-by-field
    // check as the phone number does. publicId is 22 characters of base64url (128 random bits).
    //
    // Publishing is client-written but server-checked. One transaction writes the private card,
    // the public copy (publicCards/{publicId}) and the ownership registry (publicIds/{publicId}),
    // and the rules compare them after the write, so a bug in app code is refused here:
    //   S1/S2  the registry is create-once and names its owner; only that owner may write the
    //          public copy; a card may only name an ID the registry holds for its owner.
    //   S3     Reset Link: a new ID, registered in the same write; the old public copy is gone;
    //          an ID the registry has ever held cannot be named again (retired for good).
    //   S4     a public copy is deleted only when the card no longer names it live.
    //   S5     a live card has its public copy, at the card's revision, after every write.
    //   S6     sharing needs schema 2; an older build cannot drop publicId or roll the card back.
    //   S7     a suspended or taken-down account cannot publish, re-enable or reset.
    //   P2     the public copy holds exactly the Shared, non-empty fields, equal to the private card.
    // Access calls per write stay well under Firestore's limit: at most 6 distinct documents.

    function cardLinkKeys() {
      return ['link1', 'link2', 'link3', 'link4', 'link5'];
    }

    function cardShareKeysV2() {
      return cardShareKeys().concat(cardLinkKeys());
    }

    function cardOptionalKeysV2() {
      return ['publicId', 'publishedAt'].concat(cardLinkKeys());
    }

    // 128 random bits as base64url: 22 characters.
    function validPublicId(id) {
      return id is string && id.size() == 22 && id.matches('^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{22}$');
    }

    // An additional link: a label, and an https address with no spaces and no user@ credentials.
    function validCardLink(link) {
      // Reading a missing key, or matching a value that is not text, is an error, which denies.
      return link.keys().hasOnly(['label', 'url'])
        && link.label.matches('^[^\\s\\x00-\\x1f][^\\x00-\\x1f]{0,39}$')
        && link.url.size() <= 500
        && link.url.matches('^https://[^/@?#\\s]+([/?#][^\\s]*)?$');
    }

    // A time as the service writes it: new Date().toISOString().
    function cardTime(value) {
      // matches() errors, which denies, for anything that is not text; the pattern bounds the length.
      return value.matches('^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}T[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}([.][0-9]{1,6})?Z$');
    }

    // A picture kept in the card: the JPEG data-URL prefix (23 characters), then base64 that begins as every
    // JPEG does, base64 characters only, padded to a whole number of 4s, within the bounds above.
    function validCardPhotoData(photo) {
      return photo.size() >= 200 && photo.size() <= 80000
        && (photo.size() - 23) % 4 == 0
        && photo.matches('^data:image/jpeg;base64,/9j/[A-Za-z0-9+/]+={0,2}$');
    }

    // The fields both versions share, each within its limit.
    function validCardBase(d) {
      // One match per text field: it errors (so denies) for anything that is not text, and bounds the
      // length. A line break does not match `.`, so it is refused: the app collapses all whitespace.
      return d.firstName.matches('^.{0,60}$')
        && d.lastName.matches('^.{0,60}$')
        && d.title.matches('^.{0,100}$')
        && d.organization.matches('^.{0,120}$')
        && d.email.matches('^.{0,254}$')
        && d.phone.matches('^.{0,40}$')
        && d.linkedinUrl.matches('^.{0,200}$')
        && d.instagram.matches('^.{0,30}$')
        && d.website.matches('^.{0,200}$')
        && d.photoUrl is string
        && (d.photoUrl == ''
            || (d.photoUrl.size() <= 500 && d.photoUrl.matches('^https://.+'))
            || validCardPhotoData(d.photoUrl))
        && d.share is map
        && d.share.photoUrl is bool && d.share.title is bool && d.share.organization is bool
        && d.share.email is bool && d.share.phone is bool && d.share.linkedinUrl is bool
        && d.share.instagram is bool && d.share.website is bool
        && d.sharingEnabled is bool
        && d.schemaVersion is int
        && d.revision is int
        && cardTime(d.createdAt) && cardTime(d.updatedAt);
    }

    // Schema 1: exactly the original card, sharing off.
    function validCardV1(d) {
      return d.keys().hasAll(cardKeys()) && d.keys().hasOnly(cardKeys())
        && validCardBase(d)
        && d.share.keys().hasAll(cardShareKeys()) && d.share.keys().hasOnly(cardShareKeys())
        && d.sharingEnabled == false
        && d.schemaVersion == 1;
    }

    // Schema 2: the original fields, thirteen switches, and the optional sharing fields.
    // `before` is the stored card (an empty map when there is none): a link that is unchanged was
    // checked when it was stored, and is not checked again. That keeps one write under Firestore's
    // limit of 1000 rule expressions per request, which Reset Link (four writes) comes close to.
    function validLinkSlot(d, before, slot) {
      return !(slot in d) || d[slot] == before.get(slot, null) || validCardLink(d[slot]);
    }

    function validCardV2(d, before) {
      return d.keys().hasAll(cardKeys()) && d.keys().hasOnly(cardKeys().concat(cardOptionalKeysV2()))
        && validCardBase(d)
        && d.share.size() == 13 && d.share.keys().hasOnly(cardShareKeysV2())
        && d.share.link1 is bool && d.share.link2 is bool && d.share.link3 is bool
        && d.share.link4 is bool && d.share.link5 is bool
        && d.schemaVersion == 2
        && (d.keys().hasAll(['publicId', 'publishedAt']) || !d.keys().hasAny(['publicId', 'publishedAt']))
        && (!('publicId' in d) || (validPublicId(d.publicId) && cardTime(d.publishedAt)))
        && validLinkSlot(d, before, 'link1') && validLinkSlot(d, before, 'link2') && validLinkSlot(d, before, 'link3')
        && validLinkSlot(d, before, 'link4') && validLinkSlot(d, before, 'link5')
        // A live card names its public ID.
        && (d.sharingEnabled == false || 'publicId' in d);
    }

    function validCardValues(d, before) {
      return d.get('schemaVersion', 0) == 1 ? validCardV1(d) : validCardV2(d, before);
    }

    // The stored revision as the service reads it: a whole number from 0 up,
    // and 0 for a card from before revisions (or one with a damaged value).
    function storedCardRevision(before) {
      return before.get('revision', 0) is int && before.get('revision', 0) >= 0
        ? before.get('revision', 0) : 0;
    }

    function cardDocPath(uid) {
      return /databases/$(database)/documents/users/$(uid)/card/main;
    }

    function publicCardPath(id) {
      return /databases/$(database)/documents/publicCards/$(id);
    }

    function publicIdPath(id) {
      return /databases/$(database)/documents/publicIds/$(id);
    }

    // S7: an account that is suspended, or whose card was taken down, cannot publish.
    // Both flags are server-controlled on users/{uid}, so the owner cannot write them.
    function cardPublishAllowed(uid) {
      let account = get(/databases/$(database)/documents/users/$(uid)).data;
      return account.get('accountStatus', '') != 'suspended'
        && account.get('myCardPublishBlocked', false) != true;
    }

    // The publish gate: a name and at least one Shared contact method. Additional links do not count.
    function cardPublishGate(p) {
      return (p.firstName != '' || p.lastName != '')
        && ((p.share.email == true && p.email != '')
            || (p.share.phone == true && p.phone != '')
            || (p.share.linkedinUrl == true && p.linkedinUrl != '')
            || (p.share.instagram == true && p.instagram != '')
            || (p.share.website == true && p.website != ''));
    }

    // P2: the public copy holds exactly the Shared, non-empty fields, each equal to the private value.
    // `p` is the private card AFTER this write. Equality is checked once for every key the two
    // share (diff), then presence one field at a time: in the copy exactly when its switch is on and
    // the value is not empty. A key the card has and the copy may not (share, sharingEnabled,
    // publicId, publishedAt, createdAt) is refused by the key list.
    function publicCardKeys() {
      return ['firstName', 'lastName', 'revision', 'schemaVersion', 'updatedAt'];
    }

    function publicCardMatches(d, p, id) {
      // x.diff(y): added = keys in x that y lacks; changed = keys in both whose values differ.
      let delta = d.diff(p);
      // sharingEnabled true implies schema 2 and a passed publish gate: the card rule allows nothing else.
      return p.sharingEnabled == true && p.publicId == id
        && d.keys().hasAll(publicCardKeys())
        // No key the card has and the copy may not; no key the card lacks (addedKeys); none that differs.
        && !d.keys().hasAny(['share', 'sharingEnabled', 'publicId', 'publishedAt', 'createdAt'])
        && delta.addedKeys().size() == 0 && delta.changedKeys().size() == 0
        && ('photoUrl' in d) == (p.share.photoUrl == true && p.photoUrl != '')
        && ('title' in d) == (p.share.title == true && p.title != '')
        && ('organization' in d) == (p.share.organization == true && p.organization != '')
        && ('email' in d) == (p.share.email == true && p.email != '')
        && ('phone' in d) == (p.share.phone == true && p.phone != '')
        && ('linkedinUrl' in d) == (p.share.linkedinUrl == true && p.linkedinUrl != '')
        && ('instagram' in d) == (p.share.instagram == true && p.instagram != '')
        && ('website' in d) == (p.share.website == true && p.website != '')
        && ('link1' in d) == (p.share.link1 == true && 'link1' in p)
        && ('link2' in d) == (p.share.link2 == true && 'link2' in p)
        && ('link3' in d) == (p.share.link3 == true && 'link3' in p)
        && ('link4' in d) == (p.share.link4 == true && 'link4' in p)
        && ('link5' in d) == (p.share.link5 == true && 'link5' in p);
    }

    // S1, S3, S6: the ID a card names. Unchanged, or a brand-new one that the registry did not hold
    // before this write and holds for this owner after it. Never removed once set.
    function cardIdentityOk(d, before, uid) {
      return (before.get('publicId', '') == d.get('publicId', '')
              && before.get('publishedAt', '') == d.get('publishedAt', ''))
        || ('publicId' in d && d.publicId != before.get('publicId', '')
            && !exists(publicIdPath(d.publicId))
            && getAfter(publicIdPath(d.publicId)).data.ownerUid == uid
            && (!('publishedAt' in before) || d.publishedAt == before.publishedAt));
    }

    // S3, S4, S5: the public copies agree with the card after this write.
    function cardPublicCopiesOk(d, before, uid) {
      return
        // An ID the card has left behind has no public copy.
        (!('publicId' in before) || before.publicId == d.get('publicId', '')
          || !existsAfter(publicCardPath(before.publicId)))
        // The ID it names: a public copy exists exactly while the card is live, at its revision.
        && (!('publicId' in d)
            || (d.sharingEnabled == true
                ? (cardPublishGate(d)
                   && existsAfter(publicCardPath(d.publicId))
                   && getAfter(publicCardPath(d.publicId)).data.revision == d.revision)
                : !existsAfter(publicCardPath(d.publicId))));
    }

    function validCardUpdate(d, before, uid) {
      return validCardValues(d, before)
        && d.revision == storedCardRevision(before) + 1
        // A card a LATER version of the schema wrote is not replaced by an earlier one.
        && (!(before.get('schemaVersion', 1) is number) || before.get('schemaVersion', 1) <= d.schemaVersion)
        // createdAt is set once: kept whenever the stored card has one.
        && (!(before.get('createdAt', '') is string) || before.get('createdAt', '') == ''
            || d.createdAt == before.createdAt)
        && cardIdentityOk(d, before, uid)
        && cardPublicCopiesOk(d, before, uid)
        // S7: going live, staying live or taking a new ID needs an account that may publish.
        // Turning sharing off, or saving while off under the same ID, never does.
        && ((d.sharingEnabled == false && before.get('publicId', '') == d.get('publicId', ''))
            || cardPublishAllowed(uid));
    }

    // ── Stickers (ADR-007) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

    // The rollback switch. Returning false denies every sticker write and
    // changes nothing else; scripts/rules-check/stickers.mjs runs the suite
    // against that variant too.
    function stickerRulesEnabled() {
      return true;
    }

    // The rollout mode. A missing document or any other value is "off".
    function stickersMode() {
      let flag = /databases/$(database)/documents/feature_flags/stickers;
      return exists(flag) ? get(flag).data.get('mode', 'off') : 'off';
    }

    // Platform admins, from users/{uid}.role only (a server-controlled field).
    // The ADMIN_USER_IDS environment list that other admin checks also honour
    // is invisible here, so stickersConfig does not use it either.
    function isStickerAdmin() {
      let me = /databases/$(database)/documents/users/$(request.auth.uid);
      return exists(me) && get(me).data.get('role', '') in ['admin', 'super_admin'];
    }

    function isStickerEnrolled() {
      let row = /databases/$(database)/documents/sticker_enrollment/$(request.auth.uid);
      return exists(row) && get(row).data.get('stage', '') == 'beta';
    }

    // off: nobody · internal: admins · beta: admins and enrolled users · on: everyone
    function stickerWritesAllowed() {
      let mode = stickersMode();
      return stickerRulesEnabled() && request.auth != null && (
        mode == 'on'
        || (mode == 'beta' && (isStickerAdmin() || isStickerEnrolled()))
        || (mode == 'internal' && isStickerAdmin()));
    }

    function stickerEditableFields() {
      return ['name', 'color', 'emoji', 'description', 'status', 'archived_at', 'updated_at'];
    }

    function stickerFields() {
      return stickerEditableFields().concat(['schema_version', 'created_at', 'created_by']);
    }

    function validStickerValues(d) {
      return d.name is string && d.name.size() >= 1 && d.name.size() <= 60
        && d.color in ['teal', 'blue', 'purple', 'coral', 'amber', 'pink', 'green', 'gray']
        && (d.emoji == null || (d.emoji is string && d.emoji.size() <= 16))
        && (d.description == null || (d.description is string && d.description.size() <= 280))
        && (d.status == 'active' ? d.archived_at == null : (d.status == 'archived' && d.archived_at is timestamp))
        && d.updated_at == request.time;
    }

    function validNewSticker(stickerId, d) {
      return stickerId.matches('^[A-Za-z0-9]{20}$')
        && d.keys().hasAll(stickerFields()) && d.keys().hasOnly(stickerFields())
        && d.schema_version == 1
        && d.created_by == request.auth.uid
        && d.created_at == request.time
        && d.status == 'active'
        && validStickerValues(d);
    }

    function validStickerUpdate(d, before) {
      return d.diff(before).affectedKeys().hasOnly(stickerEditableFields())
        && validStickerValues(d);
    }

    function stickerMembershipKeys() {
      return ['sticker_ids', 'sticker_assignments', 'sticker_change'];
    }

    // Does this write add, change or remove a membership field?
    function touchesStickerMembership() {
      return request.resource != null && (resource == null
        ? request.resource.data.keys().hasAny(stickerMembershipKeys())
        : request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasAny(stickerMembershipKeys()));
    }

    function activeStickerOf(userId, stickerId) {
      let path = /databases/$(database)/documents/users/$(userId)/stickers/$(stickerId);
      return stickerId is string && exists(path) && get(path).data.status == 'active';
    }

    function validAssignment(a) {
      return a is map
        && a.keys().hasAll(['assigned_at', 'assigned_by']) && a.keys().hasOnly(['assigned_at', 'assigned_by'])
        && a.assigned_at == request.time
        && a.assigned_by in ['user', 'csv_import'];
    }

    // Exactly one sticker added or removed, named in sticker_change, with one
    // assignment per sticker and no other assignment touched. At most 50.
    function validMembershipChange(userId) {
      let before = resource.data.get('sticker_ids', []);
      let after = request.resource.data.get('sticker_ids', []);
      let beforeAssignments = resource.data.get('sticker_assignments', {});
      let assignments = request.resource.data.get('sticker_assignments', {});
      let change = request.resource.data.get('sticker_change', {});
      let added = after.toSet().difference(before.toSet());
      let removed = before.toSet().difference(after.toSet());
      return after is list && after.size() <= 50 && after.toSet().size() == after.size()
        && assignments is map && assignments.keys().size() == after.size() && assignments.keys().hasAll(after)
        && change is map
        && change.keys().hasAll(['op', 'sticker_id', 'at']) && change.keys().hasOnly(['op', 'sticker_id', 'at'])
        && change.at == request.time
        && assignments.diff(beforeAssignments).affectedKeys().hasOnly([change.sticker_id])
        && ((change.op == 'add'
              && added.size() == 1 && added.hasAll([change.sticker_id]) && removed.size() == 0
              && activeStickerOf(userId, change.sticker_id)
              && validAssignment(assignments[change.sticker_id]))
          || (change.op == 'remove'
              && removed.size() == 1 && removed.hasAll([change.sticker_id]) && added.size() == 0));
    }

    // Applied to every write under users/{uid}/contacts. A write that leaves
    // the membership fields alone costs nothing extra. A new contact never
    // starts with stickers: they are added afterwards, one at a time.
    function contactStickersOk(coll, userId) {
      return coll != 'contacts'
        || !touchesStickerMembership()
        || (resource != null && stickerWritesAllowed() && validMembershipChange(userId));
    }

    match /users/{userId} {
      allow read: if owns(userId);
      allow create: if owns(userId)
        && !request.resource.data.keys().hasAny(
             serverControlledUserFields().removeAll(signupBillingFields()))
        && (!request.resource.data.keys().hasAny(signupBillingFields())
            || signupBilling(request.resource.data));
      allow update: if owns(userId)
        && !request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys()
             .hasAny(serverControlledUserFields());
      allow delete: if false;

      // Every 2-deep document under the user. `coll` is a SINGLE-segment
      // wildcard, so it is a plain string and can be compared directly — no
      // path indexing and no size(), neither of which exists for the recursive
      // wildcard binding.
      //
      // capture_operations is server-only: the capture endpoints read and
      // write it through the Admin SDK, and no client may touch it.
      //
      // integrations/linkedin is read-only to its owner: only the Connect
      // LinkedIn functions write it, after LinkedIn confirms the identity. A
      // client that could write it could mark any LinkedIn member as its own.
      // Only the trusted writer publishes this small Contact Hub display state.
      function contactPhotoCreateOk(coll) {
        return coll != 'contacts' || !request.resource.data.keys().hasAny(['contact_hub_photo', 'contact_hub_lifecycle', 'contact_hub_merge_origin', 'contact_hub_primary_phone', 'contact_hub_email_suppressed', 'contact_affiliations']);
      }
      function contactPhotoUpdateOk(coll) {
        return coll != 'contacts' || (contactIsActive(resource.data)
          && !request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasAny(['contact_hub_photo', 'contact_hub_lifecycle', 'contact_hub_merge_origin', 'contact_hub_primary_phone', 'contact_hub_email_suppressed', 'contact_affiliations']));
      }
      function contactIsActive(data) {
        return data.get('contact_hub_lifecycle', {}).get('status', '') != 'retired';
      }
      function contactPhotoDeleteOk(coll) {
        // The photo and this server-only marker are written atomically.
        // Avoid rule lookups on each ordinary contact in a bulk delete.
        return coll != 'contacts' || resource == null || (contactIsActive(resource.data) && !resource.data.keys().hasAny(['contact_hub_photo']));
      }

      match /contacts/{contactId}/private/photo {
        allow get: if owns(userId)
          && exists(/databases/$(database)/documents/users/$(userId))
          && exists(/databases/$(database)/documents/users/$(userId)/contacts/$(contactId));
        allow list, write: if false;
      }

      match /{coll}/{docId} {
        allow read:   if owns(userId) && coll != 'capture_operations' && coll != 'contact_duplicate_dismissals';
        allow create: if owns(userId) && coll != 'capture_operations' && coll != 'contact_duplicate_dismissals'
                      && coll != 'stickers'
                      && coll != 'card'
                      && !serverOwnedIntegration(coll, docId)
                      && !serverOwnedCredits(coll)
                      && contactStickersOk(coll, userId)
                      && contactPhotoCreateOk(coll);
        allow update, delete: if owns(userId)
                              && coll != 'lineageEvents'
                              && coll != 'capture_operations' && coll != 'contact_duplicate_dismissals'
                              && coll != 'stickers'
                              && coll != 'card'
                              && !serverOwnedIntegration(coll, docId)
                              && !serverOwnedCredits(coll)
                              && contactStickersOk(coll, userId)
                              && (request.resource == null ? contactPhotoDeleteOk(coll) : contactPhotoUpdateOk(coll));
      }

      // A user's stickers (ADR-007). The generic blocks exclude this
      // collection, so these are the only rules that apply to it.
      // My Card: one document, users/{uid}/card/main. The generic blocks above and below
      // skip `card`, so this is the only rule that can write it. Deleting a card is not
      // allowed (no feature deletes one; that keeps the revision history continuous), and
      // nothing under card/main can be written.
      match /card/{docId} {
        allow read:   if owns(userId);
        // A new card starts private and unpublished: nothing is shared, nor named, until an update.
        allow create: if owns(userId) && docId == 'main'
                      && validCardValues(request.resource.data, {})
                      && request.resource.data.revision == 1
                      && request.resource.data.sharingEnabled == false
                      && !request.resource.data.keys().hasAny(['publicId', 'publishedAt']);
        allow update: if owns(userId) && docId == 'main'
                      && validCardUpdate(request.resource.data, resource.data, userId);
        allow delete: if false;
      }

      match /stickers/{stickerId} {
        allow read:   if owns(userId);
        allow create: if owns(userId) && stickerWritesAllowed()
                      && validNewSticker(stickerId, request.resource.data);
        allow update: if owns(userId) && stickerWritesAllowed()
                      && validStickerUpdate(request.resource.data, resource.data);
        allow delete: if false;
      }

      // One level inside an ICP profile: criteriaVersions is append-only,
      // any other subcollection of a profile behaves normally.
      match /icpProfiles/{icpId}/{sub}/{subId} {
        allow read:   if owns(userId);
        allow create: if owns(userId);
        allow update, delete: if owns(userId) && sub != 'criteriaVersions';
      }

      // A delayed client write cannot recreate an activity or other contact
      // child after the trusted deletion commits. Allow cleanup deletes.
      function contactChildWriteOk(coll, docId) {
        return coll != 'contacts' || (existsAfter(/databases/$(database)/documents/users/$(userId)/contacts/$(docId))
          && contactIsActive(getAfter(/databases/$(database)/documents/users/$(userId)/contacts/$(docId)).data));
      }

      // Anything deeper, outside the subtrees handled above.
      //
      // Name the first nested document so the generic rule cannot grant
      // access to contact photo data through an overlapping allow.
      match /{coll}/{docId}/{sub}/{subId}/{rest=**} {
        allow read:  if owns(userId) && coll != 'capture_operations' && coll != 'contact_duplicate_dismissals'
                     && !(coll == 'contacts' && sub == 'private' && subId == 'photo');
        allow write: if owns(userId)
                     && !(coll == 'contacts' && sub == 'private' && subId == 'photo')
                     && coll != 'icpProfiles'
                     && coll != 'lineageEvents'
                     && coll != 'capture_operations' && coll != 'contact_duplicate_dismissals'
                     && coll != 'stickers'
                     && coll != 'card'
                     && !serverOwnedIntegration(coll, docId)
                     && !serverOwnedCredits(coll)
                     && contactStickersOk(coll, userId)
                     && contactChildWriteOk(coll, docId);
      }
    }

    match /users/{userId}/notifications/{notificationId} {
      allow read, write: if owns(userId);
    }

    match /apiLogs/{logId} {
      allow write: if request.auth != null;
      allow read: if false;
    }

    match /dashboards/{userId} {
      allow read: if owns(userId);
      allow write: if owns(userId);
    }

    match /impersonationSessions/{sessionId} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    // Cooldown state for utils/alertOps.js — when each scheduled job last
    // emailed OPS_ALERT_EMAIL. Written only by the Admin SDK, which bypasses
    // rules entirely, so denying every client is free.
    //
    // Stated explicitly rather than left to Firestore's default-deny. A client
    // that could write here could set lastAlertAtMs into the future and
    // silence every cron alert indefinitely, and the collection would look
    // unprotected to anyone reading this file for what is covered. Default
    // behaviour is not a decision until it is written down.
    match /ops_alerts/{job} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    // Relationship Capture, server-only like ops_alerts. capture_reports holds
    // opt-in bad-scan reports (card images and values, kept 30 days);
    // capture_enrollment is the beta rollout list, and a client that could
    // write it could enroll itself.
    match /capture_reports/{reportId} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
    match /capture_enrollment/{userId} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    // Connect LinkedIn, server-only. oauth_states holds single-use OAuth
    // states (hashed) for ten minutes; a client that could write one could
    // forge a state for another user. linkedin_member_claims records which
    // user holds each LinkedIn account; a client that could write it could
    // take someone else's.
    match /oauth_states/{stateHash} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
    match /linkedin_member_claims/{memberHash} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    // Billing hardening PR 1, all server-only like the blocks above.
    //   stripeWebhookEvents          dedup ledger — a client that could write it could fake an
    //                                 event as already applied, or clear one to force a re-apply.
    //   stripeWebhookPendingEvents   parked subscription events whose owning account wasn't yet
    //                                 known at delivery time; drained once it is.
    //   billingAllocations           "has this subscription's initial credit grant already
    //                                 happened" — a client that could write it could grant
    //                                 itself credits, or erase the marker to re-trigger a grant.
    //   billingCheckoutOperations    the durable record created at checkout time (uid, Stripe
    //                                 customer/session/subscription ids, price) that later
    //                                 webhook processing checks an incoming event against before
    //                                 trusting any identity it claims. A client that could write
    //                                 this could manufacture its own "proof" of a purchase.
    //   billingAmbiguousAccounts     append-only audit trail for anything the webhook could not
    //                                 safely auto-resolve.
    match /stripeWebhookEvents/{eventId} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
    match /stripeWebhookPendingEvents/{customerId} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
    match /stripeWebhookPendingEvents/{customerId}/events/{eventId} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
    match /billingAllocations/{subscriptionId} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
    match /billingCheckoutOperations/{operationId} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
    // Stripe Customer creation/linking is performed by the trusted billing function. A client
    // must not be able to create another account's provisioning claim or bind a Stripe ID.
    match /billingCustomerOperations/{uid} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
    match /billingAmbiguousAccounts/{docId} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    // My Card sharing (MY-CARD-SHARE-001).
    //
    // publicCards/{publicId}: the public copy of a card. Anyone may GET one whose ID they hold;
    // nobody may list, so there is no way to enumerate cards. Only the owner named by the registry
    // writes it, and only as an exact copy of the Shared, non-empty fields of their private card as
    // it stands after the same transaction (publicCardMatches). It is deleted only when the card no
    // longer names it live. Admin functions delete it through the Admin SDK, which bypasses rules.
    match /publicCards/{publicId} {
      allow get: if validPublicId(publicId);
      allow list: if false;
      allow create, update: if request.auth != null && validPublicId(publicId)
        && getAfter(publicIdPath(publicId)).data.ownerUid == request.auth.uid
        && cardPublishAllowed(request.auth.uid)
        && publicCardMatches(request.resource.data, getAfter(cardDocPath(request.auth.uid)).data, publicId);
      allow delete: if request.auth != null && validPublicId(publicId)
        && getAfter(publicIdPath(publicId)).data.ownerUid == request.auth.uid
        && !(getAfter(cardDocPath(request.auth.uid)).data.get('sharingEnabled', false) == true
             && getAfter(cardDocPath(request.auth.uid)).data.get('publicId', '') == publicId);
    }

    // publicIds/{publicId}: who owns each public ID, forever. Nobody reads it (rules read it with
    // get(), which read rules do not limit), it is written once, and no client updates or deletes
    // it, so an ID stays bound to its owner after sharing is turned off and after Reset Link, and a
    // retired ID can never be registered again. It is created only in the same transaction that
    // makes the owner's private card name that ID, so IDs cannot be claimed in advance.
    match /publicIds/{publicId} {
      allow read: if false;
      allow create: if request.auth != null && validPublicId(publicId)
        && request.resource.data.keys().hasAll(['ownerUid', 'createdAt'])
        && request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(['ownerUid', 'createdAt'])
        && request.resource.data.ownerUid == request.auth.uid
        && cardTime(request.resource.data.createdAt)
        && getAfter(cardDocPath(request.auth.uid)).data.get('publicId', '') == publicId;
      allow update, delete: if false;
    }

    // Sticker rollout (ADR-007), server-only. A client that could write
    // feature_flags/stickers could turn the feature on for itself, and one
    // that could write sticker_enrollment could enroll itself. Rules still
    // read both through get(), which read rules do not limit.
    match /feature_flags/{flag} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
    match /sticker_enrollment/{userId} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
  }
}
